package collector

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"os/exec"
	"strings"
	"time"

	"github.com/prometheus/client_golang/prometheus"
)

// CommandDuration times every Execute call, labeled by outcome ("success" or
// "error"). It is declared once at package scope, not per collector, because
// Prometheus metrics are meant to live for the process lifetime, not the
// lifetime of whichever struct happens to touch them. Exported (capitalized)
// so main.go's // @@COLLECTOR_REGISTRY@@ wiring (see registry.frag) can
// register it directly on this exporter's own custom registry.
//
// Deliberately built with plain prometheus.NewHistogramVec, not
// promauto.NewHistogramVec: promauto's factories auto-register into
// prometheus.DefaultRegisterer, the process-wide global registry, but
// main.go builds its own prometheus.NewRegistry() instead of using that
// global one (see main.go's own comment on that line), and only collectors
// reachable via register()/tracker.Add(...) ever reach /metrics. A
// *prometheus.HistogramVec already implements prometheus.Collector, so
// registry.frag registers this var the exact same way it registers every
// other collector: no promauto, no second registry to thread through.
//
// Single process-wide histogram, labeled only by outcome: if /prometheus-exporter:add-collector
// later wires a second CLI-flavor collector that runs a different command,
// both collectors' timings share these same "success"/"error" buckets and
// are not distinguishable from each other by command. Fine for this
// scaffold's one-collector v0.1 starting point; a per-command breakdown
// would need an additional label threaded through every Execute call site.
var CommandDuration = prometheus.NewHistogramVec(prometheus.HistogramOpts{
	Name: "@@NAMESPACE@@_exporter_command_duration_seconds",
	Help: "Duration in seconds of external commands issued by this exporter's collectors, by outcome.",
}, []string{"outcome"})

// CommandLimiter bounds how many invocations of the target command run at
// once. nil means unlimited, the default, so the common path costs one nil
// check. See internal/collector/limiter.go.
//
// A package-level var, not a field on some Client, because this flavor's I/O
// boundary is the package-level Execute var below, not a struct built per
// target: there is nothing to hang a per-instance limiter off of. Exported,
// and settable directly (collector.CommandLimiter = collector.NewLimiter(n)),
// the same way main.go's wiring is free to reassign Execute itself for
// tests: see Execute's own doc comment for that precedent.
var CommandLimiter *Limiter

// Execute is the I/O boundary every collector in this flavor calls through,
// instead of shelling out via os/exec directly. It is a package-level var,
// not a plain function, specifically so tests can swap it for a stub that
// returns fixture bytes (see collector_test.go's TestExampleCollector_Collect
// and TestExampleCollector_ErrorHandling): there is no live binary to run in
// CI, and this indirection is what makes a collector testable without one.
//
// Cancellation and per-command timeouts are entirely the caller's
// responsibility via ctx: see NewExampleCollector's timeout field and
// exampleData's use of context.WithTimeout for this flavor's convention.
// Threading the timeout through ctx like this, rather than Execute owning a
// package-level timeout variable internally, is more idiomatic Go (the
// caller controls cancellation, Execute just obeys it) and keeps this var
// free of any mutable package state of its own.
//
// That responsibility is enforced, not just conventional, whenever a
// concurrency ceiling is attached: with CommandLimiter non-nil, Execute
// rejects a ctx that carries no deadline instead of handing it to
// CommandLimiter.Acquire, which would otherwise block forever on a ctx that
// can never expire. A caller that never sets
// --exporter.max-requests-per-target (CommandLimiter stays nil, the
// default) is unaffected either way. See the body below for the check
// itself.
//
// Every call, success or failure, is timed and recorded on CommandDuration,
// through a deferred, named-return observation, the same shape the http
// flavor's Client.Fetch uses for RequestDuration. That symmetry matters here
// specifically: it is what makes an abandoned CommandLimiter wait visible on
// this histogram too, not just a real command's outcome. An earlier version
// observed with a plain inline call after cmd.CombinedOutput() returned,
// which skipped the observation entirely whenever CommandLimiter.Acquire
// failed and returned before CombinedOutput ever ran, making a starved
// collector invisible on CommandDuration (though still visible via logging
// and StatusTracker).
//
// Never pass a credential in args. A process's command line is world-readable
// through /proc/<pid>/cmdline, so ps exposes it to every account on the host.
// Pass it through cmd.Env, or through a 0600 file whose path is the argument.
// See references/security-and-hardening.md, Rule 1.
var Execute = func(ctx context.Context, name string, args ...string) (out []byte, err error) {
	start := time.Now()
	defer func() {
		outcome := "success"
		if err != nil {
			outcome = "error"
		}
		CommandDuration.WithLabelValues(outcome).Observe(time.Since(start).Seconds())
	}()

	// The per-command deadline is already live on ctx by the time Execute
	// runs: every caller applies it before calling Execute (see, for example,
	// exampleData's context.WithTimeout in collector.go), never inside
	// Execute itself. So the wait for a CommandLimiter slot, below, is charged
	// against that same budget rather than added on top of it.
	//
	// That is a known limitation on this flavor, not a design to copy. It
	// means a collector queued behind its siblings spends its command budget
	// waiting and can reach exec with nothing left, which is the starvation
	// the http flavor's Client.Fetch now avoids by giving the wait and the
	// request separate budgets. Closing it here needs more than a reordering:
	// the deadline arrives already applied to ctx, so Execute would have to
	// read the remaining budget off ctx.Deadline() and re-arm two budgets from
	// it, on a ctx stripped of that deadline but still cancelable by its
	// parent, which is not directly expressible (context.WithoutCancel drops
	// the cancellation too). Doable, not free, and not a reordering. Until it
	// is done, keep a ceiling on this flavor comfortably above the number of
	// collectors that can call Execute at once, or keep the per-collector
	// timeouts long enough to absorb a queue.
	//
	// Enforced here, not just documented above: a collector added later by
	// /prometheus-exporter:add-collector that forgets its own context.WithTimeout and passes a
	// bare context.Background() through would otherwise reach
	// CommandLimiter.Acquire with no bound at all, once a ceiling is
	// configured, and block forever. Checked only when CommandLimiter is
	// actually attached: with no ceiling (every deployment before v0.7, and
	// every deployment today that never sets --exporter.max-requests-per-target)
	// Acquire's own nil fast path never blocks, so an undeadlined ctx costs
	// nothing there and this check would only reject a caller for no reason.
	if CommandLimiter != nil {
		if _, ok := ctx.Deadline(); !ok {
			return nil, fmt.Errorf("execute %s: ctx has no deadline while a concurrency ceiling is configured; apply the collector's own timeout via context.WithTimeout before calling Execute", name)
		}
	}
	release, err := CommandLimiter.Acquire(ctx)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("wait for a run slot: %w", err)
	}
	defer release()

	cmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, name, args...) //nolint:gosec // G204: name is a fixed, developer-controlled command baked into each collector's own *Data method, never end-user input
	out, err = cmd.CombinedOutput()
	if err != nil {
		// A canceled/expired ctx is almost always the real cause behind the
		// generic process error CombinedOutput returns in that case (e.g.
		// "signal: killed"); surfacing ctx.Err() instead is a clearer signal
		// to callers and their logs than the raw OS-level failure would be.
		if ctxErr := ctx.Err(); ctxErr != nil {
			return nil, fmt.Errorf("execute %s: %w", name, ctxErr)
		}
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("execute %s: %w: %s", name, err, strings.TrimSpace(string(out)))
	}

	return out, nil
}
